          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01XRtcuCWg2WhcGdCErLiUQo",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/backpressure.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Backpressure\\ndescription: Mechanism by which a slow downstream consumer signals an upstream producer to reduce emission rate in streaming pipelines.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Backpressure\\n  - pattern/StreamProcessing\\n  - pattern/ConcurrentSystems\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Clear, authoritative definition with multiple concrete implementation patterns cited.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nBackpressure in a streaming pipeline is the mechanism by which a slow downstream consumer signals an upstream producer to reduce its emission rate, rather than letting an unbounded buffer grow until the process runs out of memory.\\n\\n## Common Implementations\\n\\nThree primary patterns for implementing backpressure exist:\\n\\n1. **Bounded Queues**: A fixed-size queue sits between producer and consumer. Once the queue reaches capacity, the producer blocks until space becomes available. This is simple to implement but can cause deadlocks if not carefully designed.\\n\\n2. **Credit-Based Flow Control**: The consumer explicitly grants the producer permission to send N more items. When the consumer processes items and capacity becomes available, it sends new \\u0022credits\\u0022 back upstream. This allows more fine-grained control and is common in systems like Kafka and gRPC.\\n\\n3. **Reactive-Pull Models**: Instead of the producer pushing items to the consumer, the consumer requests items on its own schedule. The producer only emits when explicitly asked, naturally preventing overload. This model underlies reactive streams libraries like RxJava and Project Reactor.\\n\\n## Why Backpressure Matters\\n\\nWithout backpressure, a fast producer can overwhelm a slow consumer, causing:\\n- Unbounded memory growth as items queue up\\n- Eventual out-of-memory errors\\n- System instability and cascading failures\\n\\nBackpressure ensures stable operation by coupling producer and consumer rates.\\n\\n## Related Concepts\\n\\n- [[concepts/flow-control]] \\u2014 broader family of rate-limiting mechanisms\\n- [[concepts/reactive-streams]] \\u2014 streams that respect backpressure signals\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
